When doing backend security review or writing backend code in this repo, use the
skill defined in `SKILL.md` at the repo root as the source of truth. Read
`SKILL.md` first for the router and mode logic, then open the relevant
`references/*.md` file(s). The router is grouped — OWASP spine, cross-cutting
surfaces, package decisions — and the "Ownership and boundaries" section below
it names the single owning file whenever two rows could both match, so read
that before assuming a topic is duplicated.
Review-time produces prioritized findings (severity,
location, CWE + OWASP mapping, an optional ASVS 5.0 chapter where the project
is held to that standard, fix); write-time applies the secure-default
contract and closes with a short security-decisions note rather than a findings
report. Each write-time rule sits beside the control it completes in the
reference file, so the file you opened for the concern already carries the rule
for writing it. The methodology, both output formats, the severity rubric
including how a race and a surviving-personal-data failure are rated, the ASVS
chapter mapping and its declared non-goals, and the rule for a secure default
that conflicts with the request are in
`references/00-methodology-and-severity.md`.

Treat the privilege model and object-/field-level authorization, impersonation
and break-glass access, OAuth2/OIDC and social login, API-key lifecycle/scoping,
agent and MCP tool surfaces, and third-party security-library vetting as
first-class review triggers. Use `agent-and-llm-interfaces.md` whenever a
backend endpoint is exposed to an agent or an LLM-driven caller. Use A01
for per-request access-control failures and for SSRF wherever a server-side
fetch takes its URL from input, and `authorization-architecture.md` for
the model behind them, `privileged-access-and-impersonation.md` for operator
privilege, A07 for auth/API keys, A03 for the dependency gate,
`data-layer-and-database.md` whenever database roles, row-level security, tenant
context on pooled connections, encrypted columns, raw SQL, NoSQL queries, read
replicas, or connection pooling are in view, `data-lifecycle-and-privacy.md`
whenever deletion, soft delete, retention, anonymization, personal-data exports,
or copies of a record in indexes, caches, files, or lower environments are in
view, `service-identity-and-secrets.md` whenever service-to-service calls,
machine tokens, JWKS, client credentials, mutual TLS, workload identity,
internal-only endpoints, secret storage or delivery, or `SECRET_KEY` rotation
are in view, `graphql-and-alternative-api-surfaces.md` whenever a GraphQL
schema, resolver, mutation, or a non-DRF framework such as Django Ninja is in
view, `api-drf-specific.md` whenever a viewset, custom `@action`, serializer,
filter backend, paginator, throttle class, OpenAPI schema route, API version,
or bulk endpoint is in view, `file-uploads.md` whenever a `FileField`, an
upload endpoint, a `STORAGES` setting, an object-store or bucket
configuration, a presigned or signed URL, a direct-to-storage upload, or a
private download or media-serving path is in view,
`a10-exceptional-conditions.md` whenever a
transaction, a lock, a database constraint, an idempotency key, a retried or
duplicated request, a state transition, a background-task dispatch, or an
error/exception handler on a security path is in view,
`a08-integrity-and-deserialization.md` whenever an inbound or outbound webhook,
an HMAC or signature check, a Celery task or broker message, a cache or session
serializer, a fixture load, or any call that turns bytes back into objects is in
view, `a04-cryptographic-failures.md` whenever a password hasher, a
`PASSWORD_HASHERS` setting, a generated token or random value, a secret
comparison, a `Signer`/`TimestampSigner` salt, an encryption key or its
rotation, or an encrypted field's key material is in view,
`a02-security-misconfiguration.md` whenever a settings module, a CORS or CSP
policy, or a DNS record carrying SPF, DKIM, DMARC, or CAA is in view,
`deployment-and-runtime.md` whenever a Dockerfile, a `.dockerignore`, a proxy
or ingress configuration, an `X-Forwarded-*` header, a client-IP lookup, a
Gunicorn or systemd unit, or a debug, profiling, metrics, or health endpoint is
in view, `a05-injection.md` whenever raw SQL, a user-supplied column or sort
key, a `subprocess` or `os.system` call, `mark_safe` or a template built from a
string, an LDAP filter, or a response or mail header built from input is in
view — it carries the inventory of every sink and the reference that owns each
one — and the security-hardening library index for dated package
dispositions.

Read-only triage scripts are in `scripts/`. Do not rely on this summary alone —
read the referenced files.
